<article><div class="video"><b>▶</b><span>2-minute video clip to be added</span></div><div><span class="eyebrow">Establishment</span><h2>What gives a newly planted tree the best start?</h2><p>Healthy establishment begins before a tree enters the ground. The planting hole, root position, finished depth, soil contact and first watering all influence how well the tree adapts.</p><p>After planting, stakes and guards should support the tree without restricting it. Watering needs to respond to weather, soil and plant size rather than follow a one-size-fits-all assumption. Regular observations make it easier to correct minor issues early.</p><strong>Video topic · Approx. 2 minutes</strong></div></article>
<article><div class="video alt"><b>▶</b><span>3-minute video clip to be added</span></div><div><span class="eyebrow">Planting at scale</span><h2>When does machine planting make sense?</h2><p>Machine planting can be valuable when the site has suitable access, workable terrain, repeatable spacing and enough volume to make mechanised delivery efficient.</p><p>It is not automatically the best method for every project. Stock type, soil condition, underground constraints and tight landscaped areas may favour manual planting. Reviewing these details early helps select a method that balances production with planting quality.</p><strong>Video topic · Approx. 3 minutes</strong></div></article>
